<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest for the MemosShare app extension.

  An .appex is its own bundle and Apple reads its manifest separately from the host app's, so
  this file declares what THIS bundle's executable can reach, which is a strict subset of the app:

    NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp — TWO keys, one from each package this bundle
    links, because the category is a claim about every file-timestamp API the executable can
    reach:

      * `.contentModificationDateKey` — SharedFile.imported() reads it off the copy it just made
        of a shared file (Packages/MemosShareKit/Sources/MemosShareKit/SharedItem.swift). The copy
        is staged in this extension's own temporary directory, so the reason is C617.1; the
        timestamp reaches the server as an upload's `last_modified`, which rules out DDA9.1.
      * `.creationDateKey` — OutboxRepository reads it off an outbox manifest whose JSON could not
        be read (Packages/MemosOutbox/Sources/MemosOutbox/OutboxRepository.swift), so that the
        capture keeps a place in capture order. MemosOutbox is linked into this bundle as well as
        into the app, so the read is declared by both. The file is the outbox's own, in the app
        group container this extension is a member of, which C617.1 covers along with the bundle's
        own container — and the value never leaves the device: it is the `capturedAt` of a job
        written `.held` with a binding that names the file rather than a server, so nothing drains
        it.

    AVAsset's `.creationDate` (Packages/MemosOutbox/Sources/MemosOutbox/Capture/CaptureReader.swift)
    is deliberately NOT in that list, for the reason the app's manifest gives: it loads an
    AVMetadataItem out of the movie's own QuickTime metadata, which is the content of the file
    rather than a timestamp the file system keeps.

    NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults — declared for what this bundle CARRIES rather than
    for what it calls. The extension asks for no preference of its own, but MemosOutbox is linked
    into it, and among the packages this extension links it is the only one that uses the API:
    the outbox's mobile-data choice
    (Packages/MemosOutbox/Sources/MemosOutbox/UploadPreferences.swift). The app has three more
    uses of its own, which is why the two manifests declare the same reasons for different code. Apple reads each
    bundle's manifest against the executable that bundle holds, so an .appex declares the reasons
    the code inside it earns. 1C8F.1 for the app group's suite the choice is kept in — the group
    this extension is a member of, which is the point of keeping it there — and CA92.1 for the
    fallback to this process's own defaults when that group is unreachable.

  Audited and deliberately NOT declared: the extension links only MemosShareKit and MemosOutbox,
  neither of which reads volume capacity, system uptime or active input modes. The extension
  carries no client, no session and no credential.

  Nothing is collected and nothing is tracked: the extension writes a capture into the app
  group's outbox and returns.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>C617.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
                <string>1C8F.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
